The frozen proof was not clean: Annex A correctly exposes the shared equal-characteristic blind spot. More importantly, a separate unannexed (A1) indexing gap remains.

### Finding 1 — GAP: the “seven families” are not families in the stated (A1) sense

> “**I (7 families):** the depth-0 W-12.A laws (m = 0).”

> “Every family is (A1)-ADMISSIBLE … one \(c_F(q)\) per family…”

But the raw terminal keys explicitly include

> “`('VERT1', u₀, k, λ)` / `('VERT2', u₀, t, λ)` / `('FULL', k, λ)`”

and (A1) requires each \(F\) to have one fixed splitting type \(\sigma_F\) and one coefficient \(c_F(q)\).

Counter-configuration: take \(q=5\), a sufficiently visible FULL depth, and compare:

- \(\lambda=(1,1,1)\): splitting type \((1,1)^3\), residual census \(S_{(1,1,1)}=(q-1)(q-2)(q-3)/6=4\).
- \(\lambda=(3)\): splitting type \((1,3)\), residual census \(S_{(3)}=(q^3-q)/3=40\).

Both belong to the stated I-FULL “family,” but they have different \(\sigma\) and different \(c(q)\). VERT1 and VERT2 similarly split between quadratic residual types \((1,1)\) and \((2)\). Tier II inherits the same problem.

PE4 came directly to this seam but misclassified it:

> “**The \(S_\lambda\) censuses are nowhere displayed in this note** … Not a defect — the note consumes tier I by pointer…”

The supplier pointer supplies the laws, but it does not make a λ-varying schema into an (A1) family with fixed \(\sigma_F\).

This also invalidates the literal family counts. The natural formal refinement has:

- I: \(4+2+2+3=11\), not 7.
- II: 11, not 7.
- B: 24 after the already-admitted \(m=0\)/\(m\ge1\) split.
- TRP total: 46; full menu total: \(46+4+3=53\).

Consequently, both

> “≤ 45 TRP aggregate families + 4 DBL + 3 SEP”

and the acceptance record’s

> “complete 33-family cubic core menu”

are not correct as formal (A1) family counts. They count schemas while calling them families. The density arithmetic may survive a finite refinement, but that refinement is not stated or annexed.

### Finding 2 — GAP, resolved only by Annex A: the accepting passes certified a false lift quantifier

Frozen theorem:

> “so Ore certifies **EVERY lift**”

Frozen proof:

> “assigns the SAME \(\sigma\) to every \(O\)-lift **(not only disc ≠ 0 ones)**”

PE4 explicitly targeted and endorsed that assertion:

> “(A0) as consumed claims **EVERY lift** of every menu member carries the family \(\sigma\)”

Counter-configuration: let \(O=\mathbf F_3[[t]]\), take the RAM3(1) window member with \(b_2=b_1=0\) and \(b_0=t\), and choose the lift
\[
F(y)=y^3+t.
\]
It is conservatively classified by the RAM3(1) Newton data, but \(F'(y)=0\) and \(\operatorname{disc}(F)=0\). It is purely inseparable and has no separable splitting type of the kind used by \(R_\tau\).

PE4’s “wildcard” checked only mixed-characteristic \(\mathbf Z_p\) lifts, so it could not detect this. Thus PE3/PE4’s zero-gap verdicts were false for the frozen post-r2 body. Annex A correctly restricts (A0) to disc-\(\ne0\) lifts, which is the form W-12.D actually consumes.

### Finding 3 — MINOR: Annex A misdescribes its own logical effect

> “This annex changes no statement’s content and no proof step”

The second clause is supported: the stronger quantifier was not needed downstream. The first is literally false. Replacing “every lift” by “every disc-\(\ne0\) lift” is a strict weakening, as the \(\mathbf F_3[[t]]\), \(y^3+t\) counterexample demonstrates. The accurate conclusion is that the annex changes the frozen statement while preserving the intended (A0) clause and all downstream conclusions.

